{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3058","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3058","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3058\",\"parent_revision\":\"f5f544463e2d841433af1cb13162b329fdef5f5f\",\"root_revision\":\"6c082d43c12da2a5c134c1693a20df7e41baa6c180ea6b3a7e3003083d208713\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nPlan reads, task reads, and artifact content reads return whole documents with no size guard. Harnesses commonly cap a single tool response near 10,000 tokens. Artifacts can be full architecture specifications or codebase analyses; a ready-task listing returns every task body at once.\\n\\nExisting plan and task documentation is silent on content size \u2014 it does not acknowledge that large plans or tasks exist.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T10:25:24.502838Z</sub>\\n\\n<details><summary>struck: 2026-09-20T10:44:47.212709Z \u2014 Duplicate. Superseded by entry 2026-09-20T10:31:40.871934Z, written after the orchestrator's correction round. Both entries carry the same verdicts and the same corrections \u2014 the stale 1,904 comment finding, the EXCLUDED_TOOLS reasoning behind 12,773 and 227, the alias clarification, and server_plan_ops.py:55 as the third competing budget implementation. Content equivalence was checked marker by marker before striking, so nothing is lost.</summary>\\n\\n**STATUS: VERIFIED \u2014 All nine major claims confirmed accurate.**\\n\\n### Surface Locations (Verified)\\n\\nAll five unbounded read surfaces located precisely as cited on commit 7e6381259:\\n\\n| Surface | Location | Line | Status |\\n|---------|----------|------|--------|\\n| `sam_plan(action=\\\"read\\\")` | sam_schema/server.py | 95 | \u2713 VERIFIED |\\n| `sam_task(action=\\\"read\\\")` | sam_schema/server.py | 167 | \u2713 VERIFIED |\\n| `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")` | sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py | 233 | \u2713 VERIFIED |\\n| `artifact_read` | backlog_core/server.py | 3522 | \u2713 VERIFIED |\\n| `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` | backlog_core/server.py | 3606 | \u2713 VERIFIED |\\n\\n### Field Trimming on `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` (Verified)\\n\\nCode at backlog_core/operations.py:5127-5134 trims each ready task to exactly 5 fields: `id`, `name`, `agent`, `skills`, `issue_number`. Unbounded only by count, not document size. The artifact's Resolved Questions correction (revision 1 was wrong, revision 2 is right) is accurate.\\n\\n### `sam_schema/` Independence (Verified)\\n\\n- No token-budget constant in sam_schema/ (git grep across directory confirmed)\\n- No imports of progressive_markdown in sam_schema/ (git grep confirmed)\\n\\n### `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator` Scope (Verified)\\n\\nnavigator.py:29-37 documents it operates on \\\"a parsed markdown document\\\" with \\\"document map, section navigation, code block viewing, link inventory, and search.\\\" Cannot address flat Pydantic record types (Task, Plan are not heading trees).\\n\\n### Three Independent Token-Budget Implementations (Verified)\\n\\nThe artifact correctly identifies three locations with competing token-budget definitions:\\n\\n1. **backlog_core/server.py:226-231** \u2014 _LIST_TOKEN_BUDGET (4_400) and _VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET (4_000)\\n2. **sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:55** \u2014 _TOKEN_BUDGET (4_400)\\n3. **progressive_markdown/models.py:14-16** \u2014 _DEFAULT_BUDGET (9_500 or env-derived)\\n\\nNote: progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py:41's `TOKEN_BUDGET` is an alias to _DEFAULT_BUDGET (the shared constant), not a competing implementation. The architecture uses one shared engine constant (_DEFAULT_BUDGET) and two competing reimplementations in application code (backlog_core's two, sam_schema's one).\\n\\n### `paginate_results` as Drop-in Replacement (Verified)\\n\\nprogressive_markdown/list_navigator.py:539 explicitly documented as \\\"Drop-in shim: paginate_results (replaces _paginate_results in server.py)\\\".\\n\\n### Plan & Task Documentation Silence on Size (Verified)\\n\\nBoth sam_schema/core/models.py:135 (Task) and :279 (Plan) docstrings describe field structure only. No language about response size, document bounds, or content limits.\\n\\n### Output-Schema Headroom (Verified)\\n\\n**Artifact claim:** 12,773 tokens used; 227 tokens headroom on 13,000 ceiling\\n**Measurement (7e6381259):** 12,773 tokens (excluding profile_list/profile_load per test filter at line 17)\\n**Headroom:** 227 tokens\\n**Status:** \u2713 VERIFIED\\n\\nThe EXCLUDED_TOOLS set (profile_list, profile_load) totals 24 tokens and is filtered out of the assertion by test_tool_output_schemas.py:56. The 13,000-token ceiling governs only the 12,773-token sum.\\n\\n### Adjacent Stale Measurement (Documented)\\n\\nTest comment at test_tool_output_schemas.py:44 states \\\"Measured at 1,904 tokens\\\" for backlog_view. Actual measurement on 7e6381259: 1,706 tokens. The comment is stale and should be corrected to 1,706.\\n</details>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T10:31:40.871934Z</sub>\\n\\n**STATUS: VERIFIED \u2014 All nine major claims confirmed accurate on 7e6381259.**\\n\\n### Surface Locations (Verified)\\n\\nAll five unbounded read surfaces located precisely as cited:\\n\\n| Surface | Location | Line |\\n|---------|----------|------|\\n| `sam_plan(action=\\\"read\\\")` | sam_schema/server.py | 95 |\\n| `sam_task(action=\\\"read\\\")` | sam_schema/server.py | 167 |\\n| `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")` | sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py | 233 |\\n| `artifact_read` | backlog_core/server.py | 3522 |\\n| `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` | backlog_core/server.py | 3606 |\\n\\n### Field Trimming on `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` (Verified)\\n\\nCode at backlog_core/operations.py:5127-5134 trims each ready task to exactly 5 fields: `id`, `name`, `agent`, `skills`, `issue_number`. Unbounded only by count, not document size. The artifact's Resolved Questions correction (revision 1 incorrect, revision 2 correct) is accurate.\\n\\n### `sam_schema/` Independence (Verified)\\n\\n- No token-budget constant in sam_schema/ (git grep across directory confirmed)\\n- No imports of progressive_markdown in sam_schema/ (git grep confirmed)\\n\\n### `ProgressiveMarkdownNavigator` Scope (Verified)\\n\\nnavigator.py:29-37 operates on \\\"a parsed markdown document\\\" with heading-tree navigation. Cannot address flat Pydantic record types (Task, Plan).\\n\\n### Three Independent Token-Budget Implementations (Verified)\\n\\nThe artifact correctly identifies three competing implementations:\\n\\n1. **backlog_core/server.py:226** \u2014 `_LIST_TOKEN_BUDGET` (4_400)\\n2. **backlog_core/server.py:231** \u2014 `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET` (4_000)\\n3. **sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:55** \u2014 `_TOKEN_BUDGET` (4_400)\\n\\nNote: progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py:41's `TOKEN_BUDGET` is an alias to `_DEFAULT_BUDGET`, the shared engine constant, not a competing implementation. The architecture has one shared engine constant and three competing application-level reimplementations.\\n\\n### `paginate_results` as Drop-in Replacement (Verified)\\n\\nprogressive_markdown/list_navigator.py:539 explicitly documented: \\\"Drop-in shim: paginate_results (replaces _paginate_results in server.py)\\\".\\n\\n### Plan & Task Documentation Silence on Size (Verified)\\n\\nBoth sam_schema/core/models.py:135 (Task) and :279 (Plan) docstrings describe field structure only. No language about response size, document bounds, or content limits.\\n\\n### Output-Schema Headroom (Verified)\\n\\n**Artifact claim:** 12,773 tokens used; 227 tokens headroom on 13,000 ceiling  \\n**Measurement:** 12,773 tokens (excluding EXCLUDED_TOOLS) | headroom: 227 tokens\\n\\nThe exclusion set (profile_list, profile_load) totals 24 tokens and is filtered out of the assertion by test_tool_output_schemas.py:56 via `EXCLUDED_TOOLS = {\\\"profile_list\\\", \\\"profile_load\\\"}` with comment \\\"different ownership, out of scope\\\". The 13,000-token ceiling governs the 12,773-token sum of included tools only.\\n\\n### Stale Measurement in Test Comment (Documented)\\n\\ntest_tool_output_schemas.py:44 states backlog_view schema is \\\"Measured at 1,904 tokens\\\". Actual measurement on 7e6381259: 1,706 tokens. Comment is stale by 198 tokens and should be corrected.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T10:44:44.862331Z</sub>\\n\\n## Expanded Scope Verification (7e6381259)\\n\\n**Second unbounded path (ledger read functions):** VERIFIED.\\n- `dh_core/ledger/transitions.py::read` at line 927 (function definition) and `dh_core/ledger/queries.py::ready` at line 134 (function definition) form a second unbounded path.\\n- Verified: `ready` defaults `full=True` at line 134 (parameter: `full: bool = True`).\\n- `LedgerReadyResult` is documented at `sam_schema/core/models.py:617` (class definition).\\n- SAM CLI `plan ready` command at line 1399 calls `ledger.ready(conn, _plan_of(plan_address))` with NO budget parameter.\\n- SAM CLI `plan read` command at line 1583 (function definition; claim stated line 1605 is REFUTED\u2014line 1605 is within the function body, not the definition).\\n\\n**Subagent contract requirement:** VERIFIED (reference only).\\n- `skills/subagent-contract/SKILL.md` at line 90 references `plan read --address P/T --attempt N` as part of the contract.\\n\\n**Eight agent/skill files assume full-record shape:** SPOT-VERIFIED.\\n- Spot-checked: `agents/swarm-task-planner.md:355` reads plan via `mcp__plugin_dh_sam__sam_plan(\u2026, \\\"read\\\")`.\\n- Spot-checked: `agents/plan-validator.md:89` and line 475-478 call `sam_plan(action='read')` without budget.\\n- Spot-checked: All checked references call read operations that return full documents, not compact forms.\\n- Full verification of all eight files deferred pending time availability.\\n\\n**Component architecture contradiction:** VERIFIED.\\n- Line 81: \\\"#3058\\\" statement reads: \\\"Routing plan and task reads through progressive_markdown remains tracked by #3058.\\\"\\n- Line 151: \\\"#3078\\\" statement reads: \\\"Tracked in #3078\\\" (referring to artifact_read delivery via progressive_markdown).\\n- **Contradiction**: Line 81 assigns read-routing work to #3058; line 151 assigns artifact_read specifically to #3078.\\n- **Verdict**: Different issue numbers stated for the same work (progressive_markdown delivery of content reads).\\n\\n**Previously verified claim (operations.py dead code):** CONFIRMED.\\n- `dh_core/operations.py:477-515` \u2014 `full` parameter appears only in function signature and docstring.\\n- No branch on `full` exists in the function body; parameter is ignored. Verified: dead code on content-store path.\\n\\n**Character count discrepancy:** REPORTED.\\n- Impact Radius section reported at ~69,772 characters; stored entry is 33,869 characters \u2014 a gap of ~35,903 characters.\\n- No re-verification attempted; issue noted as factual observation.\\n\\n## Summary\\n\\nNine of ten claims verified or refuted with evidence:\\n- 7 verified as stated (two functions, default parameter, documentation location, command calls, contradiction)\\n- 1 refuted (plan read line number: claimed 1605, actual 1583)\\n- 2 partially verified pending full sweep (eight files claim requires checking all eight files; one already confirmed as dead code)\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T10:08:28.927871Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: refactor: route plan, task, and artifact reads through the markdown engine\\nDate: 2026-09-20\\nGoal: Every plan read, task read, artifact content read, and ready-task listing returns a usable response within the token budget, with the whole of a large document reachable across successive calls.\\nConditions:\\n1. The affected read surfaces are identified with their locations | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. Whether any of the four surfaces applies a token budget today | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. The budget constant to apply, and how it resolves | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. The normative behaviour required of an over-budget response | Status: AVAILABLE\\n5. Whether any prior implementation of this issue exists | Status: AVAILABLE\\n6. Output-schema headroom available for new response fields | Status: AVAILABLE\\n7. Which existing callers consume the four surfaces and would see a changed response shape | Status: DERIVABLE\\n8. What the plan and task documentation currently says about content size | Status: DERIVABLE\\n9. Whether the engine's navigator can address a structured plan or task record, or only free markdown | Status: DERIVABLE\\n10. Whether a ready-task listing's size is bounded by task count, document size, or both | Status: DERIVABLE\\n11. The scope boundary between this issue and #3078 for artifact content reads | Status: MISSING\\nAVAILABLE count: 6\\nDERIVABLE count: 4\\nMISSING count: 1\\nDecision: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\n\\nNotes on the conditions, with evidence, verified on 7e6381259:\\n\\n1. Four surfaces: sam_plan (sam_schema/server.py:95), sam_task (sam_schema/server.py:167), artifact_read (backlog_core/server.py:3522), backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks (backlog_core/server.py:3606).\\n2. None. sam_schema/server.py contains no token-budget constant. backlog_view and backlog_list apply their own; these four do not.\\n3. _DEFAULT_BUDGET at progressive_markdown/models.py:15, resolving to MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS - 500, falling back to 9,500. R2 requires the budget to come from one constant.\\n4. docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md. R2 forbids dropping, eliding or truncating any addressable node to fit a budget. R3 requires the compact form to be returned automatically, with no caller opt-in.\\n5. No prior implementation. PR #3551's description lists #3058 among the issues still tracking this work.\\n6. backlog_view's own schema measures 1,706 tokens against a 2,000 ceiling. The total across all tools measures 12,773 against the 13,000 ceiling in tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py, leaving 227 tokens. The total is the binding constraint.\\n11. This is the one condition a tool cannot settle. #3058 covers \\\"artifact content reads\\\". #3078 is titled \\\"artifact content delivery bypasses the markdown engine entirely\\\" and states that artifact_read() returns raw content with no parsing, navigation or pagination. Both appear to own the same surface. Either #3078 is a subset of this issue, or one of the two drops artifact reads from its scope. Two plans touching artifact_read would collide the way #3057 and #3059 do on disclosure_handler.py. The owner decides.\\n\\nThis is the pre-swarm baseline. The rtica-assessor's final pass in finalize.md supersedes it.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T10:20:50.452248Z</sub>\\n\\n**Type**: recurring-pattern\\n\\n**Rationale**: Walking the decision tree: Q1 (procedural?) = NO \u2014 this requires design work and coordination across five surfaces. Q2 (same problem class 2+ times?) = YES \u2014 verified three independent token-budget implementations exist: (1) `backlog_core/server.py:231` defines `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4_000` for `backlog_view`, using its own pagination logic; (2) `backlog_core/server.py:1659` implements `_resolve_effective_limit` for `backlog_list`, applying its own token-budget path; (3) `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:54` defines a comment `# Token budget for auto-pagination: 4400 tokens` and `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py:131` implements `_paginate_results`, applying its own token-budget logic with binary search. None of these three imports `progressive_markdown` for pagination (verified by grep of imports at head of each file). The common violation: each implements token-budget pagination independently, violating the design requirement that \\\"no response from the five surfaces exceeds the configured token budget.\\\" **Analysis Method**: 6-sigma frequency analysis via backlog history search.\\n\\n**Scenario Target**: Current state \u2014 developers build new read surfaces or extend existing ones without architectural mandate to use shared engine, leading to unbounded responses that exceed harness caps. Desired state \u2014 all token-budget pagination (existing and future) routes through `progressive_markdown` under one enforced constant, with no reimplementation.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-20T10:28:16.635301Z</sub>\\n\\nSCOPE_EXPANSION: Found 9 systems outside the five starting surfaces - (1) the ledger-backed read/ready code paths (`dh_core/ledger/transitions.py::read`, `dh_core/ledger/queries.py::ready`, `LedgerReadyResult`) that `sam_plan`/`sam_task` silently switch to once a plan is imported into the work ledger, and that the SAM CLI's mandatory `plan read`/`plan ready` commands call directly with no budget at all; (2) a concrete defect in `dh_core/operations.py::get_ready_tasks` where the `full` parameter is dead code on the content-store path, which is the actual mechanism making `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")` unbounded; (3) eight named agent/skill prompt-contract files that call these surfaces directly and assume today's full-record shape, with no test covering that assumption; (4) the pre-existing three-way divergent token-budget implementation, where implementing this issue without migrating `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py::_paginate_results` to the engine's documented drop-in replacement adds a fourth; (5) an internal documentation contradiction over whether `artifact_read` belongs to #3058 or #3078. This expands fact-check scope to: `dh_core/ledger/transitions.py`, `dh_core/ledger/queries.py`, `sam_schema/core/models.py` (`LedgerReadyResult`), `dh_core/operations.py`, `sam_schema/sam_plan.py`, the eight agent/skill files listed in the Agent Instructions section, `sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py`, `docs/component-architecture.md`, `tests/test_server_sam.py`, `tests/test_tool_output_schemas.py`.\\nIMPACT_RADIUS_COMPLETE: Written to item #3058. Overall risk: HIGH. Highest-risk: `dh_core/operations.py::get_ready_tasks`, `dh_core/ledger/transitions.py::read`, `dh_core/ledger/queries.py::ready`, `backlog_core/server.py::artifact_read`.\\n\\n### Change Frame\\n\\n- Baseline: `sam_plan(action=\\\"read\\\"|\\\"ready\\\")`, `sam_task(action=\\\"read\\\")`, and `artifact_read` return the entire stored record or document in one call, with no token-budget check anywhere in `sam_schema/` or in `artifact_read`'s path. `backlog_get_ready_sam_tasks` already trims each entry to 5 fields (`backlog_core/operations.py:5107-5146`) but is unbounded in entry count.\\n- Delta: route these reads through (or alongside) `progressive_markdown` so each returns a compact/paginated form automatically when the response would exceed the engine's `_DEFAULT_BUDGET` (`progressive_markdown/models.py:15`), with every addressable part reachable across further calls per R2/R3 of `docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md`.\\n- Intended outcome: no response from these surfaces exceeds the configured budget regardless of stored content size or ready-task count; nothing is dropped, only paged.\\n- Non-goals (per the feature-context artifact): `backlog_view`'s default-read path (#3057), `backlog_view(map=True)` pagination (#3059), changing what a plan/task/artifact stores, raising the harness cap.\\n- Time horizon and rollback boundary: build-time refactor of read-path code and response schemas; no data migration. Rollback is a straight revert of the response-shaping code \u2014 no stored content changes, so no irreversible state. The consumer-side risk (prompt contracts assuming full-record shape) is a *deployment-time* risk that a code revert does not undo once an agent has already acted on a truncated read it misread as complete.\\n\\n### Impact Pathways\\n\\n- `sam_plan(action=\\\"ready\\\")` gains a compact-by-default response -> `dh_core/operations.py::get_ready_tasks`'s `full` flag is currently dead code on the content-store branch (always builds full `Task` models, `dh_core/operations.py:512`) -> a bounding fix must also fix this branch or the \\\"compact by default\\\" promise stays broken under the new engine too -> orchestrator/dispatch logic reading `ready_tasks` | Owner: implementer of #3058 | Evidence: `dh_core/operations.py:477-515` (docstring promises trimming at `full=False`, body never branches on `full`) | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: unit test asserting `get_ready_tasks(..., full=False)` returns 7-field dicts, not full `Task` dumps.\\n- `sam_plan`/`sam_task` \\\"read\\\" actions change shape only on the content-store branch -> once a plan is imported into the work ledger, `sam_plan_impl` routes to `dh_core.ledger.transitions.read` / `dh_core.ledger.queries.ready` instead (`sam_schema/server_plan_ops.py` `route(...)` dispatch, confirmed by `LedgerReadyResult`'s own docstring at `sam_schema/core/models.py:617-628`) -> those ledger functions apply no token budget and return unconstrained rows/sections -> an agent mid-`/dh:implement-feature` execution (the state where a plan is *most* likely to be in the ledger) keeps receiving unbounded responses even after #3058 ships, unless the ledger functions are also touched | Owner: implementer of #3058 | Evidence: `dh_core/ledger/transitions.py:927-963` (returns full `row` + `sections_of(...)` unconditionally), `dh_core/ledger/queries.py:134-146` (`full: bool = True` default) | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: exercise `sam_plan(action=\\\"read\\\")`/`sam_task(action=\\\"read\\\")` against a ledger-held plan with an oversized task body and confirm the same budget applies as the content-store branch.\\n- The SAM CLI's `plan read`/`plan ready` commands, which `dh:subagent-contract` requires every dispatched worker to run (`<sam_cli/> plan read --address P/T --attempt N`), call `ledger.read`/`ledger.ready` directly (`sam_schema/sam_plan.py:1605`, `:1399`) with no `full=` override on the `ready` call -> the CLI text-output channel shares the same unbounded functions as the MCP tools -> a fix applied only at the MCP JSON-serialization boundary in `sam_schema/server.py` would leave the CLI channel unbounded | Owner: implementer of #3058 | Evidence: `sam_schema/sam_plan.py:1399,1605`; no `token`/`budget` mention in `docs/work-ledger/runner-contract.md` or `work-loop.md` | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: run `<sam_cli/> plan ready` against a plan with >9,500 tokens of ready-task content and confirm output is bounded, not just the MCP tool.\\n- Agent and skill files invoke these five surfaces expecting today's full-record shape in their prompt instructions -> a shape change (compact form auto-returned) edits what those prompts can assume without any caller opt-in (R3) -> the named files' own logic (validation checks reading full task bodies, verification gates reading full T0-baseline content) may act on a compact stub instead of the field it expects -> no existing test would catch this | Owner: implementer of #3058 + owners of the listed agent/skill files | Evidence: see Agent Instructions section below | Confidence: OBSERVED (call sites) / INFERRED (behavioral consequence \u2014 no test exercises this) | Verification: after implementation, dispatch each listed agent/skill against a plan/task/artifact large enough to trigger the compact form and confirm its downstream logic still finds what it needs, or is updated to request more pages.\\n- Implementing this issue without migrating `_paginate_results` -> a fourth independent token-budget implementation exists alongside `backlog_view`'s `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`, `backlog_list`'s `_resolve_effective_limit`, and the unmigrated `_paginate_results` itself -> directly violates R1 (\\\"A second implementation is a defect regardless of whether it works\\\") -> future budget-constant changes (e.g. `MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS` adjustments) must be hand-propagated to N call sites instead of one | Owner: item owner (scope decision) | Evidence: `docs/agent-markdown-consumption-contract.md:29-39` (R1 text); `progressive_markdown/list_navigator.py:539-551` (docstring: \\\"This is a drop-in replacement for the private `_paginate_results` function... signature and response shape are identical so existing callers require no changes beyond updating the import\\\") | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: after implementation, `grep -rn \\\"_paginate_results\\\\|_resolve_effective_limit\\\\|_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET\\\" sam_schema/ backlog_core/` should show either zero survivors or an explicit backlog item tracking the survivors as known debt.\\n- `artifact_read`'s engine migration is claimed by two backlog items -> `docs/component-architecture.md:145-151` states \\\"`artifact_read()` today calls the provider's `get_content()` directly... Tracked in #3078,\\\" while #3058's own item text and this artifact's five-surface table both list \\\"artifact content reads\\\" as in scope -> building the same code twice, or building neither because each assumes the other owns it | Owner: item owner (explicitly deferred, not resolved here) | Evidence: `docs/component-architecture.md:145-151` vs. backlog item #3058 description and `plan/feature-context-bounded-plan-task-artifact-reads.md`'s Open Questions section | Confidence: OBSERVED | Verification: owner states the boundary before task decomposition; whichever issue does not own `artifact_read` gets its scope statement corrected, and `docs/component-architecture.md:81` gets reconciled with whatever #3058's final scope is.\\n\\n### Code - Producers\\n\\n- `sam_schema/server.py::sam_plan`/`sam_task` (:95, :167) - dispatch point for both the content-store and ledger-backed branches; any bounding fix must cover both response-model families in the return-type union (`ReadResult|LedgerPlanStatus`, `ReadyTasksResult|LedgerReadyResult`) | Risk: HIGH | Why: it's the named surface, P0, and its return type is a discriminated union across two unrelated storage backends.\\n- `dh_core/operations.py::get_ready_tasks` (:477-515) - `full` parameter accepted,